## an10.6
an10.6:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then Ven. Ānanda went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to the Blessed One,
an10.6:1.2 | S: “Could it be, sir, that a mendicant might gain a state of immersion like this? They wouldn’t perceive earth in earth, water in water, fire in fire, or air in air. And they wouldn’t perceive the dimension of infinite space in the dimension of infinite space, the dimension of infinite consciousness in the dimension of infinite consciousness, the dimension of nothingness in the dimension of nothingness, or the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. And they wouldn’t perceive this world in this world, or the other world in the other world. And yet they would still perceive.” | T: “Lord, could a monk have an attainment of concentration such that he would neither be percipient of earth with regard to earth, nor of water with regard to water, nor of fire… wind… the dimension of the infinitude of space… the dimension of the infinitude of consciousness… the dimension of nothingness… the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception… this world… nor of the next world with regard to the next world, and yet he would still be percipient?”
an10.6:1.3 | S: “It could be, Ānanda, that a mendicant might gain a state of immersion like this. They wouldn’t perceive earth in earth, water in water, fire in fire, or air in air. And they wouldn’t perceive the dimension of infinite space in the dimension of infinite space, the dimension of infinite consciousness in the dimension of infinite consciousness, the dimension of nothingness in the dimension of nothingness, or the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. And they wouldn’t perceive this world in this world, or the other world in the other world. And yet they would still perceive.” | T: “Yes, Ānanda, he could.…”
an10.6:2.1 | S: “But how could this be, sir?” | T: “But how, lord, could a monk have an attainment of concentration such that he would neither be percipient of earth with regard to earth…nor of the next world with regard to the next world, and yet he would still be percipient?”
an10.6:3.1 | S: “Ānanda, it’s when a mendicant perceives: | T: “There is the case, Ānanda, where the monk would be percipient in this way:
an10.6:3.2 | S: ‘This is peaceful; this is sublime—that is, the stilling of all activities, the letting go of all attachments, the ending of craving, fading away, cessation, extinguishment.’ | T: ‘This is peace, this is exquisite—the pacification of all fabrications; the relinquishing of all acquisitions; the ending of craving; dispassion; cessation; unbinding.’
an10.6:3.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant might gain a state of immersion like this. They wouldn’t perceive earth in earth, water in water, fire in fire, or air in air. And they wouldn’t perceive the dimension of infinite space in the dimension of infinite space, the dimension of infinite consciousness in the dimension of infinite consciousness, the dimension of nothingness in the dimension of nothingness, or the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. And they wouldn’t perceive this world in this world, or the other world in the other world. And yet they would still perceive.” | T: It’s in this way that a monk could have an attainment of concentration such that he would neither be percipient of earth with regard to earth, nor of water with regard to water, nor of fire… wind… the dimension of the infinitude of space… the dimension of the infinitude of consciousness… the dimension of nothingness… the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception… this world… nor of the next world with regard to the next world, and yet he would still be percipient.”
an10.6:3.4 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.71
an10.71:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
an10.71:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There he addressed the monks,
an10.71:1.3 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Monks!”
an10.71:1.4 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, lord,” the monks responded to him.
an10.71:1.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said:
an10.71:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, live by the ethical precepts and the monastic code. Live restrained in the monastic code, conducting yourselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, keep the rules you’ve undertaken. | T: “Monks, dwell consummate in virtue, consummate in terms of the Pāṭimokkha. Dwell restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in your behavior & sphere of activity. Train yourselves, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults.
an10.71:3.1 | S: A mendicant might wish: ‘May I be liked and approved by my spiritual companions, respected and admired.’ So let them fulfill their precepts, be committed to inner serenity of the heart, not neglect absorption, be endowed with discernment, and frequent empty huts. | T: [1] “If a monk would wish, ‘May I be dear & pleasing to my companions in the holy life, respected by & inspiring to them,’ then he should be one who brings the precepts to perfection, who is committed to inner tranquility of awareness, who doesn’t neglect jhāna, who is endowed with insight, and who frequents empty dwellings.
an10.71:4.1 | S: A mendicant might wish: ‘May I receive robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick.’ So let them fulfill their precepts, be committed to inner serenity of the heart, not neglect absorption, be endowed with discernment, and frequent empty huts. | T: [2] “If a monk would wish, ‘May I be someone who receives robes, alms food, lodgings, & medicinal requisites for curing the sick,’ then he should be one who brings the precepts to perfection, who is committed to inner tranquility of awareness, who doesn’t neglect jhāna, who is endowed with insight, and who frequents empty dwellings.
an10.71:5.1 | S: A mendicant might wish: ‘May the services of those whose robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick I enjoy be very fruitful and beneficial for them.’ So let them fulfill their precepts … | T: [3] “If a monk would wish, ‘Whatever I use or consume in terms of robes, alms food, lodgings, & medical requisites for curing the sick, may that be of great fruit, of great benefit to those who provided them,’ then he should be one who brings the precepts to perfection, who is committed to inner tranquility of awareness, who doesn’t neglect jhāna, who is endowed with insight, and who frequents empty dwellings.
an10.71:6.1 | S: A mendicant might wish: ‘When deceased family and relatives who have passed away recollect me with a confident mind, may this be very fruitful and beneficial for them.’ So let them fulfill their precepts … | T: [4] “If a monk would wish, ‘When my kinsmen & relatives who have died & passed away recollect me with brightened minds, may it be of great fruit, of great benefit,’ then he should be one who brings the precepts to perfection, who is committed to inner tranquility of awareness, who doesn’t neglect jhāna, who is endowed with insight, and who frequents empty dwellings.
an10.71:7.1 | S: A mendicant might wish: ‘May I be content with any kind of robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick.’ So let them fulfill their precepts … | T: [5] “If a monk would wish, ‘May I be content with whatever robes, alms food, lodgings, & medical requisites for curing the sick are available,’ then he should be one who brings the precepts to perfection, who is committed to inner tranquility of awareness, who doesn’t neglect jhāna, who is endowed with insight, and who frequents empty dwellings.
an10.71:8.1 | S: A mendicant might wish: ‘May I endure cold, heat, hunger, and thirst. May I endure the touch of flies, mosquitoes, wind, sun, and reptiles. May I endure rude and unwelcome criticism. And may I put up with physical pain—sharp, severe, acute, unpleasant, disagreeable, and life-threatening.’ So let them fulfill their precepts … | T: [6] “If a monk would wish, ‘May I be resistant to cold, heat, hunger, & thirst; to the touch of gadflies & mosquitoes, wind & sun & creeping things; to abusive, hurtful language; to bodily feelings that, when they arise, are painful, sharp, stabbing, fierce, distasteful, deadly,’ then he should be one who brings the precepts to perfection, who is committed to inner tranquility of awareness, who doesn’t neglect jhāna, who is endowed with insight, and who frequents empty dwellings.
an10.71:9.1 | S: A mendicant might wish: ‘May I prevail over desire and discontent, and may desire and discontent not prevail over me. May I live having mastered desire and discontent whenever they have arisen.’ So let them fulfill their precepts … | T: [7] “If a monk would wish, ‘May I overcome displeasure & delight, and not be overcome by displeasure & delight. May I dwell conquering again & again any displeasure & delight that has arisen,’ then he should be one who brings the precepts to perfection, who is committed to inner tranquility of awareness, who doesn’t neglect jhāna, who is endowed with insight, and who frequents empty dwellings.
an10.71:10.1 | S: A mendicant might wish: ‘May I prevail over fear and dread, and may fear and dread not prevail over me. May I live having mastered fear and dread whenever they arise.’ So let them fulfill their precepts … | T: [8] “If a monk would wish, ‘May I overcome fear & dread, and not be overcome by fear & dread. May I dwell conquering again & again any fear & dread that have arisen,’ then he should be one who brings the precepts to perfection, who is committed to inner tranquility of awareness, who doesn’t neglect jhāna, who is endowed with insight, and who frequents empty dwellings.
an10.71:11.1 | S: A mendicant might wish: ‘May I get the four absorptions—blissful meditations in this life that belong to the higher mind—when I want, without trouble or difficulty.’ So let them fulfill their precepts … | T: [9] “If a monk would wish, ‘May I attain—whenever I want, without strain, without difficulty—the four jhānas that are heightened mental states, pleasant abidings in the here & now,’ then he should be one who brings the precepts to perfection, who is committed to inner tranquility of awareness, who doesn’t neglect jhāna, who is endowed with insight, and who frequents empty dwellings.
an10.71:12.1 | S: A mendicant might wish: ‘May I realize the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom, and live having realized it with my own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements.’ So let them fulfill their precepts, be committed to inner serenity of the heart, not neglect absorption, be endowed with discernment, and frequent empty huts. | T: [10] “If a monk would wish, ‘May I—with the ending of effluents—remain in the effluent-free awareness-release & discernment-release, having directly known & realized them for myself right in the here & now,’ then he should be one who brings the precepts to perfection, who is committed to inner tranquility of awareness, who doesn’t neglect jhāna, who is endowed with insight, and who frequents empty dwellings.
an10.71:13.1 | S: ‘Live by the ethical precepts and the monastic code. Live restrained in the monastic code, conducting yourselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, keep the rules you’ve undertaken.’ | T: “‘Monks, dwell consummate in virtue, consummate in terms of the Pāṭimokkha. Dwell restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in your behavior & sphere of activity. Train yourselves, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults.’
an10.71:13.2 | S: That’s what I said, and this is why I said it.” | T: Thus was it said. And in reference to this was it said.”
an10.71:13.3 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.73
an10.73:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, these ten likable, desirable, and agreeable things are rare in the world. | T: “Monks, these ten things are welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:1.2 | S: What ten? | T: Which ten?
an10.73:1.3 | S: Wealth, | T: “Wealth is welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:1.4 | S: beauty, | T: “Beauty is welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:1.5 | S: health, | T: “Freedom from disease is welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:1.6 | S: ethical conduct, | T: “Virtues are welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:1.7 | S: the spiritual life, | T: “Celibacy is welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:1.8 | S: friends, | T: “Friends are welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:1.9 | S: learning, | T: “Learning is welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:1.10 | S: wisdom, | T: “Discernment is welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:1.11 | S: good qualities, | T: “The Dhamma is welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:1.12 | S: and heaven are likable, desirable, and agreeable things that are rare in the world. | T: “Heaven is welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world. “These, monks, are ten things that are welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:2.1 | S: Ten things are roadblocks for these ten likable, desirable, and agreeable things that are rare in the world. | T: “These ten (other) things are obstacles to these ten things that are welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:2.2 | S: Sloth and lack of initiative are a roadblock for wealth. Lack of adornment and decoration are a roadblock for beauty. Unsuitable activity is a roadblock for health. Bad friendship is a roadblock for ethical conduct. Lack of sense restraint is a roadblock for the spiritual life. Dishonesty is a roadblock for friends. Not rehearsing is a roadblock for learning. Not wanting to listen and ask questions are roadblocks for wisdom. Lack of commitment and reviewing are roadblocks for good qualities. Wrong practice hinders heaven. | T: “Indolence & lack of initiative are obstacles to wealth. “Lack of adornment & lack of embellishment are obstacles to beauty. “Unsuitable actions are obstacles to freedom from disease. “Evil friendships are obstacles to virtues. “Lack of sense restraint is an obstacle to celibacy. “Duplicity is an obstacle to friendships. “Lack of repetition is an obstacle to learning. “An unwillingness to listen or to engage in questioning is an obstacle to discernment. “Lack of commitment & lack of reflection are obstacles to Dhammas. “Wrong practice is an obstacle to heaven.
an10.73:2.3 | S: These ten things are roadblocks for these ten likable, desirable, and agreeable things that are rare in the world. | T: “These ten (other) things are obstacles to these ten things that are welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:3.1 | S: Ten things nourish these ten likable, desirable, and agreeable things that are rare in the world. | T: “These ten (other) things are food for these ten things that are welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:3.2 | S: Application and initiative nourish wealth. Adornment and decoration nourish beauty. Suitable activity nourishes health. Good friendship nourishes ethical conduct. Sense restraint nourishes the spiritual life. Honesty nourishes friends. Rehearsing nourishes learning. Eagerness to listen and ask questions nourishes wisdom. Commitment and reviewing nourish good qualities. Right practice nourishes heaven. | T: “Non-indolence & initiative are food for wealth. “Adornment & embellishment are food for beauty. “Suitable actions are food for freedom from disease. “Admirable friendships are food for virtues. “Sense restraint is food for celibacy. “Lack of duplicity is food for friendships. “Repetition is food for learning. “A willingness to listen & to engage in questioning is food for discernment. “Commitment & reflection are food for Dhammas. “Right practice is food for heaven.
an10.73:3.3 | S: These ten things nourish these ten likable, desirable, and agreeable things that are rare in the world.” | T: “These ten (other) things are food for these ten things that are welcome, agreeable, pleasant, & hard to obtain in the world.
an10.73:3.4 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.7
an10.7:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda went up to Venerable Sāriputta, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Ven. Ānanda went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an10.7:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to Sāriputta: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Sāriputta,
an10.7:2.1 | S: “Could it be, reverend Sāriputta, that a mendicant might gain a state of immersion like this? They wouldn’t perceive earth in earth, water in water, fire in fire, or air in air. And they wouldn’t perceive the dimension of infinite space in the dimension of infinite space, the dimension of infinite consciousness in the dimension of infinite consciousness, the dimension of nothingness in the dimension of nothingness, or the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. And they wouldn’t perceive this world in this world, or the other world in the other world. And yet they would still perceive.” | T: “Friend Sāriputta, could a monk have an attainment of concentration such that he would neither be percipient of earth with regard to earth, nor of water with regard to water, nor of fire… wind… the dimension of the infinitude of space… the dimension of the infinitude of consciousness… the dimension of nothingness… the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception… this world… nor of the next world with regard to the next world, and yet he would still be percipient?”
an10.7:3.1 | S: “It could be, Reverend Ānanda.” | T: “Yes, friend Ānanda, he could.…”
an10.7:4.1 | S: “But how could this be?” | T: “But how, friend Sāriputta, could a monk have an attainment of concentration such that he would neither be percipient of earth with regard to earth…nor of the next world with regard to the next world, and yet he would still be percipient?”
an10.7:4.2 | S: “Reverend Ānanda, this one time I was staying right here at Sāvatthī in the Dark Forest. | T: “Once, friend Ānanda, when I was staying right here near Sāvatthī in the Grove of the Blind,
an10.7:4.3 | S: There I gained a state of immersion like this. I didn’t perceive earth in earth, water in water, fire in fire, or air in air. And I didn’t perceive the dimension of infinite space in the dimension of infinite space, the dimension of infinite consciousness in the dimension of infinite consciousness, the dimension of nothingness in the dimension of nothingness, or the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. And I didn’t perceive this world in this world, or the other world in the other world. And yet I still perceived.” | T: I reached concentration in such a way that I was neither percipient of earth with regard to earth…nor of the next world with regard to the next world, and yet I was still percipient.”
an10.7:5.1 | S: “But at that time what did Reverend Sāriputta perceive?” | T: “But what, friend Sāriputta, were you percipient of at that time?”
an10.7:5.2 | S: “One perception arose in me and another perception ceased: ‘The cessation of continued existence is extinguishment. The cessation of continued existence is extinguishment.’ | T: “‘The cessation of becoming—unbinding—the cessation of becoming—unbinding’: One perception arose in me, friend Ānanda, as another perception ceased.
an10.7:5.3 | S: Suppose there was a burning pile of twigs. One flame would arise and another would cease. | T: Just as in a blazing woodchip fire, one flame arises as another flame ceases,
an10.7:5.4 | S: In the same way, one perception arose in me and another perception ceased: ‘The cessation of continued existence is extinguishment. The cessation of continued existence is extinguishment.’ | T: even so, ‘The cessation of becoming—unbinding—the cessation of becoming—unbinding’: One perception arose in me as another one ceased.
an10.7:5.5 | S: At that time I perceived that the cessation of continued existence is extinguishment.” | T: I was percipient at that time of ‘The cessation of becoming—unbinding.’”
an10.7:5.6 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.80
an10.80:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these ten ways to get rid of resentment. | T: “There are these ten ways of subduing hatred.
an10.80:1.2 | S: What ten? | T: Which ten?
an10.80:1.3 | S: Thinking: ‘They harmed me, but what can I possibly do?’ you get rid of resentment. | T: [1] “Thinking, ‘He has done me harm. But what should I expect?’ one subdues hatred.
an10.80:1.4 | S: Thinking: ‘They are harming me …’ … | T: [2] “Thinking, ‘He is doing me harm. But what should I expect?’ one subdues hatred.
an10.80:1.5 | S: ‘They will harm me …’ … | T: [3] “Thinking, ‘He is going to do me harm. But what should I expect?’ one subdues hatred.
an10.80:1.6 | S: ‘They harmed someone I love …’ … | T: [4] “Thinking, ‘He has done harm to people who are dear & pleasing to me. But what should I expect?’ one subdues hatred.
an10.80:1.7 | S: ‘They are harming someone I love …’ | T: [5] “Thinking, ‘He is doing harm to people who are dear & pleasing to me. But what should I expect?’ one subdues hatred.
an10.80:1.8 | S: ‘They will harm someone I love …’ … They helped someone I dislike …’ … | T: [6] “Thinking, ‘He is going to do harm to people who are dear & pleasing to me. But what should I expect?’ one subdues hatred. [7] “Thinking, ‘He has aided people who are not dear or pleasing to me. But what should I expect?’ one subdues hatred.
an10.80:1.9 | S: ‘They are helping someone I dislike …’ … | T: [8] “Thinking, ‘He is aiding people who are not dear or pleasing to me. But what should I expect?’ one subdues hatred.
an10.80:1.10 | S: Thinking: ‘They will help someone I dislike, but what can I possibly do?’ you get rid of resentment. | T: [9] “Thinking, ‘He is going to aid people who are not dear or pleasing to me. But what should I expect?’ one subdues hatred.
an10.80:1.11 | S: And you don’t get angry for no reason. | T: [10] “One does not get worked up over impossibilities.
an10.80:1.12 | S: These are the ten ways of getting rid of resentment.” | T: “These are ten ways of subduing hatred.”
an10.80:1.13 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
an10.80:1.14 | P: Ākaṅkhavaggo tatiyo. | T: (none)
an10.80:2.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
an10.80:2.1 | P: Ākaṅkho kaṇṭako iṭṭhā, | T: (none)
an10.80:2.2 | P: vaḍḍhi ca migasālāya; | T: (none)
an10.80:2.3 | P: Tayo dhammā ca kāko ca, | T: (none)
an10.80:2.4 | P: nigaṇṭhā dve ca āghātāti. | T: (none)

## an10.81
an10.81:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Campā on the banks of the Gaggarā Lotus Pond. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Campā on the shore of Gaggarā Lake.
an10.81:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Bāhuna went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then Ven. Vāhuna went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
an10.81:1.3 | S: “Sir, how many things has the Realized One escaped from, so that he lives detached, liberated, with mind free of boundaries?” | T: “Lord, freed, dissociated, & released from how many things does the Tathāgata dwell with unrestricted awareness?”
an10.81:2.1 | S: “Bāhuna, the Realized One has escaped from ten things, so that he lives detached, liberated, with mind free of boundaries. | T: “Freed, dissociated, & released from ten things, Vāhuna, the Tathāgata dwells with unrestricted awareness.
an10.81:2.2 | S: What ten? | T: Which ten?
an10.81:2.3 | S: Form … | T: Freed, dissociated, & released from form, the Tathāgata dwells with unrestricted awareness.
an10.81:2.4 | S: feeling … | T: Freed, dissociated, & released from feeling…
an10.81:2.5 | S: perception … | T: Freed, dissociated, & released from perception…
an10.81:2.6 | S: choices … | T: Freed, dissociated, & released from fabrications…
an10.81:2.7 | S: consciousness … | T: Freed, dissociated, & released from consciousness…
an10.81:2.8 | S: rebirth … | T: Freed, dissociated, & released from birth…
an10.81:2.9 | S: old age … | T: Freed, dissociated, & released from aging…
an10.81:2.10 | S: death … | T: Freed, dissociated, & released from death…
an10.81:2.11 | S: suffering … | T: Freed, dissociated, & released from stress…
an10.81:2.12 | S: defilements … | T: Freed, dissociated, & released from defilement, the Tathāgata dwells with unrestricted awareness.
an10.81:2.13 | S: Suppose there was a blue water lily, or a pink or white lotus. Though it sprouted and grew in the water, it would rise up above the water and stand with no water clinging to it. | T: “Just as a red, blue, or white lotus born in the water and growing in the water, rises up above the water and stands with no water adhering to it,
an10.81:2.14 | S: In the same way, the Realized One has escaped from ten things, so that he lives detached, liberated, with mind free of boundaries.” | T: in the same way the Tathāgata—freed, dissociated, & released from these ten things—dwells with unrestricted awareness.”
an10.81:2.15 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an10.92
an10.92:1.1 | S: Then the householder Anāthapiṇḍika went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him: | T: Then Anāthapiṇḍika the householder went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to the Blessed One, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, the Blessed One said to him,
an10.92:2.1 | S: “Householder, when a noble disciple has quelled five fears and enmities, possesses the four factors of stream-entry, and has clearly seen and penetrated the noble system with wisdom, they may, if they wish, declare of themselves: | T: “When, for a disciple of the noble ones, five forms of fear & animosity are stilled; when he is endowed with the four factors of stream entry; and when, through discernment, he has rightly seen & rightly ferreted out the noble method, then if he wants he may state about himself:
an10.92:2.2 | S: ‘I’ve finished with rebirth in hell, the animal realm, and the ghost realm. I’ve finished with all places of loss, bad places, the underworld. I am a stream-enterer! I’m not liable to be reborn in the underworld, and am assured, destined for awakening.’ | T: ‘Hell is ended for me; animal wombs are ended; the state of the hungry ghosts is ended; planes of deprivation, the bad destinations, the lower realms are ended! I am a stream-winner, never again destined for the lower realms, certain, headed for self-awakening!’
an10.92:3.1 | S: What are the five fears and enmities they have quelled? | T: “Now, which five forms of danger & animosity are stilled?
an10.92:3.2 | S: Anyone who kills living creatures brims with fear and enmity both in this life and in lives to come, and experiences mental pain and sadness. Anyone who refrains from killing living creatures does not brim with fear and enmity either in this life or in lives to come, and doesn’t experience mental pain and sadness. | T: “When a person takes life, then with the taking of life as a requisite condition, he produces fear & animosity in the here & now, produces fear & animosity in future lives, experiences mental concomitants of pain & despair; but when he refrains from taking life, he neither produces fear & animosity in the here & now nor does he produce fear & animosity in future lives, nor does he experience mental concomitants of pain & despair:
an10.92:3.3 | S: So that fear and enmity is quelled for anyone who refrains from killing living creatures. | T: For one who refrains from taking life, that fear & animosity is thus stilled.
an10.92:4.1 | S: Anyone who steals … | T: “When a person steals…
an10.92:4.2 | S: Anyone who commits sexual misconduct … | T: engages in illicit sex…
an10.92:4.3 | S: Anyone who lies … | T: tells lies…
an10.92:4.4 | S: Anyone who consumes beer, wine, and liquor intoxicants brims with fear and enmity both in this life and in lives to come, and experiences mental pain and sadness. Anyone who refrains from beer, wine, and liquor intoxicants does not brim with fear and enmity either in this life or in lives to come, and doesn’t experience mental pain and sadness. | T: “When a person drinks distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness, then with the drinking of distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness as a requisite condition, he produces fear & animosity in the here & now, produces fear & animosity in future lives, experiences mental concomitants of pain & despair; but when he refrains from drinking distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness, he neither produces fear & animosity in the here & now nor does he produce fear & animosity in future lives, nor does he experience mental concomitants of pain & despair:
an10.92:4.5 | S: So that fear and enmity is quelled for anyone who refrains from beer, wine, and liquor intoxicants. | T: For one who refrains from drinking distilled & fermented drinks that cause heedlessness, that fear & animosity is thus stilled.
an10.92:4.6 | S: These are the five fears and enmities they have quelled. | T: “These are the five forms of fear & animosity that are stilled.
an10.92:5.1 | S: What are the four factors of stream-entry that they possess? | T: “And which are the four factors of stream entry with which he is endowed?
an10.92:5.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple has experiential confidence in the Buddha: | T: “There is the case where the disciple of the noble ones is endowed with verified confidence in the Awakened One:
an10.92:5.3 | S: ‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ | T: ‘Indeed, the Blessed One is worthy & rightly self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, well-gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed.’
an10.92:5.4 | S: They have experiential confidence in the teaching: | T: “He is endowed with verified confidence in the Dhamma:
an10.92:5.5 | S: ‘The teaching is well explained by the Buddha—apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves.’ | T: ‘The Dhamma is well taught by the Blessed One, to be seen here & now, timeless, inviting verification, pertinent, to be experienced by the observant for themselves.’
an10.92:5.6 | S: They have experiential confidence in the Saṅgha: | T: “He is endowed with verified confidence in the Saṅgha:
an10.92:5.7 | S: ‘The Saṅgha of the Buddha’s disciples is practicing the way that’s good, direct, systematic, and proper. It consists of the four pairs, the eight individual persons. This is the Saṅgha of the Buddha’s disciples that is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of greeting with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world.’ | T: ‘The Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples who have practiced well… who have practiced straight-forwardly… who have practiced methodically…who have practiced masterfully—in other words, the four pairs, the eight individuals—they are the Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples: deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, the incomparable field of merit for the world.’
an10.92:5.8 | S: And a noble disciple’s ethical conduct is loved by the noble ones, unbroken, impeccable, spotless, and unmarred, liberating, praised by sensible people, not mistaken, and leading to immersion. | T: “He is endowed with virtues that are appealing to the noble ones: untorn, unbroken, unspotted, unsplattered, liberating, praised by the observant, ungrasped at, leading to concentration.
an10.92:5.9 | S: These are the four factors of stream-entry that they possess. | T: “These are the four factors of stream entry with which he is endowed.
an10.92:6.1 | S: And what is the noble system that they have clearly seen and penetrated with wisdom? | T: “And which is the noble method that he has rightly seen & rightly ferreted out through discernment?
an10.92:6.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple reflects: | T: “There is the case where a disciple of the noble ones notices:
an10.92:6.3 | S: ‘So, when this exists, this comes to be; due to the arising of this, this arises. | T: “When this is, that is. “From the arising of this comes the arising of that.
an10.92:6.4 | S: When this doesn’t exist, this doesn’t come to be; due to the cessation of this, this ceases. That is: | T: “When this isn’t, that isn’t. “From the cessation of this comes the cessation of that. “In other words:
an10.92:6.5 | S: Ignorance is a requirement for choices. | T: “From ignorance as a requisite condition come fabrications.
an10.92:6.6 | S: Choices are a requirement for consciousness. | T: “From fabrications as a requisite condition comes consciousness.
an10.92:6.7 | S: Consciousness is a requirement for name and form. | T: “From consciousness as a requisite condition comes name-&-form.
an10.92:6.8 | S: Name and form are requirements for the six sense fields. | T: “From name-&-form as a requisite condition come the six sense media.
an10.92:6.9 | S: The six sense fields are requirements for contact. | T: “From the six sense media as a requisite condition comes contact.
an10.92:6.10 | S: Contact is a requirement for feeling. | T: “From contact as a requisite condition comes feeling.
an10.92:6.11 | S: Feeling is a requirement for craving. | T: “From feeling as a requisite condition comes craving.
an10.92:6.12 | S: Craving is a requirement for grasping. | T: “From craving as a requisite condition comes clinging/sustenance.
an10.92:6.13 | S: Grasping is a requirement for continued existence. | T: “From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming.
an10.92:6.14 | S: Continued existence is a requirement for rebirth. | T: “From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth.
an10.92:6.15 | S: Rebirth is a requirement for old age and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress to come to be. | T: “From birth as a requisite condition, then aging & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
an10.92:6.16 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
an10.92:6.17 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases no residue left behind, choices cease. When choices cease, consciousness ceases. When consciousness ceases, name and form cease. When name and form cease, the six sense fields cease. When the six sense fields cease, contact ceases. When contact ceases, feeling ceases. When feeling ceases, craving ceases. When craving ceases, grasping ceases. When grasping ceases, continued existence ceases. When continued existence ceases, rebirth ceases. When rebirth ceases, old age and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress cease. | T: “Now from the remainderless fading & cessation of that very ignorance comes the cessation of fabrications. From the cessation of fabrications comes the cessation of consciousness. From the cessation of consciousness comes the cessation of name-&-form. From the cessation of name-&-form comes the cessation of the six sense media. From the cessation of the six sense media comes the cessation of contact. From the cessation of contact comes the cessation of feeling. From the cessation of feeling comes the cessation of craving. From the cessation of craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance. From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
an10.92:6.18 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.’ | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
an10.92:6.19 | S: This is the noble system that they have clearly seen and penetrated with wisdom. | T: “This is the noble method that he has rightly seen & rightly ferreted out through discernment.
an10.92:7.1 | S: When a noble disciple has quelled five fears and enmities, possesses the four factors of stream-entry, and has clearly seen and penetrated the noble system with wisdom, they may, if they wish, declare of themselves: | T: “When, for a disciple of the noble ones, these five forms of fear & animosity are stilled; when he is endowed with these four factors of stream entry; and when, through discernment, he has rightly seen & rightly ferreted out this noble method, then if he wants he may state about himself:
an10.92:7.2 | S: ‘I’ve finished with rebirth in hell, the animal realm, and the ghost realm. I’ve finished with all places of loss, bad places, the underworld. I am a stream-enterer! I’m not liable to be reborn in the underworld, and am assured, destined for awakening.’” | T: ‘Hell is ended for me; animal wombs are ended; the state of the hungry ghosts is ended; planes of deprivation, the bad destinations, the lower realms are ended! I am a stream-winner, never again destined for the lower realms, certain, headed for self-awakening!’”
an10.92:7.3 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an11.11
an11.11:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Sakyans, near Kapilavatthu in the Banyan Tree Monastery. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Sakyans at Kapilavatthu in the Banyan Park.
an11.11:1.2 | S: At that time several mendicants were making a robe for the Buddha, thinking that | T: Now at that time many monks were at work making robes for the Blessed One, (thinking,)
an11.11:1.3 | S: when his robe was finished and the three months of the rains residence had passed the Buddha would set out wandering. | T: “When the robes are finished, at the end of the three months (of the Rains retreat), the Blessed One will set out wandering.”
an11.11:1.4 | S: Mahānāma the Sakyan heard about this. | T: Mahānāma the Sakyan heard that
an11.11:1.5 | P: “sambahulā kira bhikkhū bhagavato cīvarakammaṁ karonti: | T: many monks were at work making robes for the Blessed One,
an11.11:1.6 | P: ‘niṭṭhitacīvaro bhagavā temāsaccayena cārikaṁ pakkamissatī’”ti. | T: (thinking,) “When the robes are finished, at the end of the three months, the Blessed One will set out wandering.”
an11.11:2.1 | S: He went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: So he approached the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
an11.11:2.2 | S: “Sir, I have heard that | T: “I have heard that
an11.11:2.3 | S: several mendicants are making a robe for the Buddha, thinking that | T: many monks are at work making robes for the Blessed One, (thinking,)
an11.11:2.4 | S: when his robe was finished and the three months of the rains residence had passed the Buddha would set out wandering. | T: ‘When the robes are finished, at the end of the three months, the Blessed One will set out wandering.’
an11.11:2.5 | S: Now, we spend our life in various ways. Which of these should we practice?” | T: For those of us living by means of various dwelling places (for the mind), by means of which dwelling place should we live?”
an11.11:3.1 | S: “Good, good, Mahānāma! | T: “Excellent, Mahānāma, excellent!
an11.11:3.2 | S: It’s appropriate that gentlemen such as you come to me and ask: | T: It is fitting for clansmen like you to approach the Tathāgata and ask,
an11.11:3.3 | S: ‘We spend our life in various ways. Which of these should we practice?’ | T: ‘For those of us living by means of various dwelling places (for the mind), by means of which dwelling place should we live?’
an11.11:3.4 | S: The faithful succeed, not the faithless. | T: “One who is aroused to practice is one of conviction, not without conviction.
an11.11:3.5 | S: The energetic succeed, not the lazy. | T: One aroused to practice is one with persistence aroused, not lazy.
an11.11:3.6 | S: The mindful succeed, not the unmindful. | T: One aroused to practice is one of established mindfulness, not muddled mindfulness.
an11.11:3.7 | S: Those with immersion succeed, not those without immersion. | T: One aroused to practice is centered in concentration, not uncentered.
an11.11:3.8 | S: The wise succeed, not the witless. | T: One aroused to practice is discerning, not undiscerning.
an11.11:3.9 | S: When you’re grounded on these five things, go on to develop six further things. | T: “Established in these five qualities, you should further develop six qualities:
an11.11:3.10 | S: Firstly, you should recollect the Realized One: | T: [1] “There is the case where you recollect the Tathāgata:
an11.11:3.11 | S: ‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ | T: ‘Indeed, the Blessed One is worthy & rightly self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, well-gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed.’
an11.11:3.12 | S: When a noble disciple recollects the Realized One their mind is not full of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: At any time when a disciple of the noble ones is recollecting the Tathāgata, his mind is not overcome with passion, not overcome with aversion, not overcome with delusion.
an11.11:3.13 | S: At that time their mind is quite unswerving, based on the Realized One. | T: His mind heads straight, based on the Tathāgata.
an11.11:3.14 | S: A noble disciple whose mind is unswerving finds inspiration in the meaning and the teaching, and finds joy connected with the teaching. | T: And when the mind is headed straight, the disciple of the noble ones gains a sense of the goal, gains a sense of the Dhamma, gains joy connected with the Dhamma.
an11.11:3.15 | S: When they’re joyful, rapture springs up. When the mind is full of rapture, the body becomes tranquil. When the body is tranquil, they feel bliss. And when they’re blissful, the mind becomes immersed in samādhi. | T: In one who is joyful, rapture arises. In one whose mind is enraptured, the body grows calm. One whose body is calmed experiences ease. In one at ease, the mind becomes concentrated.
an11.11:3.16 | S: This is called a noble disciple who lives in balance among people who are unbalanced, and lives untroubled among people who are troubled. They’ve entered the stream of the teaching and developed the recollection of the Buddha. | T: “Of one who does this, Mahānāma, it is said: ‘Among those who are out of tune, the disciple of the noble ones dwells in tune; among those who are malicious, he dwells without malice; having attained the stream of Dhamma, he develops the recollection of the Buddha.’
an11.11:4.1 | S: Furthermore, you should recollect the teaching: | T: [2] “Then there is the case where you recollect the Dhamma:

