 succeeded in 0ms:
an10.92:6.13 | S: Grasping is a requirement for continued existence. | T: “From clinging/sustenance as a requisite condition comes becoming.
an10.92:6.14 | S: Continued existence is a requirement for rebirth. | T: “From becoming as a requisite condition comes birth.
an10.92:6.15 | S: Rebirth is a requirement for old age and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress to come to be. | T: “From birth as a requisite condition, then aging & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair come into play.
an10.92:6.16 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering originates. | T: Such is the origination of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
an10.92:6.17 | S: When ignorance fades away and ceases no residue left behind, choices cease. When choices cease, consciousness ceases. When consciousness ceases, name and form cease. When name and form cease, the six sense fields cease. When the six sense fields cease, contact ceases. When contact ceases, feeling ceases. When feeling ceases, craving ceases. When craving ceases, grasping ceases. When grasping ceases, continued existence ceases. When continued existence ceases, rebirth ceases. When rebirth ceases, old age and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress cease. | T: “Now from the remainderless fading & cessation of that very ignorance comes the cessation of fabrications. From the cessation of fabrications comes the cessation of consciousness. From the cessation of consciousness comes the cessation of name-&-form. From the cessation of name-&-form comes the cessation of the six sense media. From the cessation of the six sense media comes the cessation of contact. From the cessation of contact comes the cessation of feeling. From the cessation of feeling comes the cessation of craving. From the cessation of craving comes the cessation of clinging/sustenance. From the cessation of clinging/sustenance comes the cessation of becoming. From the cessation of becoming comes the cessation of birth. From the cessation of birth, then aging & death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair all cease.
an10.92:6.18 | S: That is how this entire mass of suffering ceases.’ | T: Such is the cessation of this entire mass of stress & suffering.
an10.92:6.19 | S: This is the noble system that they have clearly seen and penetrated with wisdom. | T: “This is the noble method that he has rightly seen & rightly ferreted out through discernment.
an10.92:7.1 | S: When a noble disciple has quelled five fears and enmities, possesses the four factors of stream-entry, and has clearly seen and penetrated the noble system with wisdom, they may, if they wish, declare of themselves: | T: “When, for a disciple of the noble ones, these five forms of fear & animosity are stilled; when he is endowed with these four factors of stream entry; and when, through discernment, he has rightly seen & rightly ferreted out this noble method, then if he wants he may state about himself:
an10.92:7.2 | S: ‘I’ve finished with rebirth in hell, the animal realm, and the ghost realm. I’ve finished with all places of loss, bad places, the underworld. I am a stream-enterer! I’m not liable to be reborn in the underworld, and am assured, destined for awakening.’” | T: ‘Hell is ended for me; animal wombs are ended; the state of the hungry ghosts is ended; planes of deprivation, the bad destinations, the lower realms are ended! I am a stream-winner, never again destined for the lower realms, certain, headed for self-awakening!’”
an10.92:7.3 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an11.11
an11.11:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Sakyans, near Kapilavatthu in the Banyan Tree Monastery. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Sakyans at Kapilavatthu in the Banyan Park.
an11.11:1.2 | S: At that time several mendicants were making a robe for the Buddha, thinking that | T: Now at that time many monks were at work making robes for the Blessed One, (thinking,)
an11.11:1.3 | S: when his robe was finished and the three months of the rains residence had passed the Buddha would set out wandering. | T: “When the robes are finished, at the end of the three months (of the Rains retreat), the Blessed One will set out wandering.”
an11.11:1.4 | S: Mahānāma the Sakyan heard about this. | T: Mahānāma the Sakyan heard that
an11.11:1.5 | P: “sambahulā kira bhikkhū bhagavato cīvarakammaṁ karonti: | T: many monks were at work making robes for the Blessed One,
an11.11:1.6 | P: ‘niṭṭhitacīvaro bhagavā temāsaccayena cārikaṁ pakkamissatī’”ti. | T: (thinking,) “When the robes are finished, at the end of the three months, the Blessed One will set out wandering.”
an11.11:2.1 | S: He went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: So he approached the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
an11.11:2.2 | S: “Sir, I have heard that | T: “I have heard that
an11.11:2.3 | S: several mendicants are making a robe for the Buddha, thinking that | T: many monks are at work making robes for the Blessed One, (thinking,)
an11.11:2.4 | S: when his robe was finished and the three months of the rains residence had passed the Buddha would set out wandering. | T: ‘When the robes are finished, at the end of the three months, the Blessed One will set out wandering.’
an11.11:2.5 | S: Now, we spend our life in various ways. Which of these should we practice?” | T: For those of us living by means of various dwelling places (for the mind), by means of which dwelling place should we live?”
an11.11:3.1 | S: “Good, good, Mahānāma! | T: “Excellent, Mahānāma, excellent!
an11.11:3.2 | S: It’s appropriate that gentlemen such as you come to me and ask: | T: It is fitting for clansmen like you to approach the Tathāgata and ask,
an11.11:3.3 | S: ‘We spend our life in various ways. Which of these should we practice?’ | T: ‘For those of us living by means of various dwelling places (for the mind), by means of which dwelling place should we live?’
an11.11:3.4 | S: The faithful succeed, not the faithless. | T: “One who is aroused to practice is one of conviction, not without conviction.
an11.11:3.5 | S: The energetic succeed, not the lazy. | T: One aroused to practice is one with persistence aroused, not lazy.
an11.11:3.6 | S: The mindful succeed, not the unmindful. | T: One aroused to practice is one of established mindfulness, not muddled mindfulness.
an11.11:3.7 | S: Those with immersion succeed, not those without immersion. | T: One aroused to practice is centered in concentration, not uncentered.
an11.11:3.8 | S: The wise succeed, not the witless. | T: One aroused to practice is discerning, not undiscerning.
an11.11:3.9 | S: When you’re grounded on these five things, go on to develop six further things. | T: “Established in these five qualities, you should further develop six qualities:
an11.11:3.10 | S: Firstly, you should recollect the Realized One: | T: [1] “There is the case where you recollect the Tathāgata:
an11.11:3.11 | S: ‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ | T: ‘Indeed, the Blessed One is worthy & rightly self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, well-gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed.’
an11.11:3.12 | S: When a noble disciple recollects the Realized One their mind is not full of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: At any time when a disciple of the noble ones is recollecting the Tathāgata, his mind is not overcome with passion, not overcome with aversion, not overcome with delusion.
an11.11:3.13 | S: At that time their mind is quite unswerving, based on the Realized One. | T: His mind heads straight, based on the Tathāgata.
an11.11:3.14 | S: A noble disciple whose mind is unswerving finds inspiration in the meaning and the teaching, and finds joy connected with the teaching. | T: And when the mind is headed straight, the disciple of the noble ones gains a sense of the goal, gains a sense of the Dhamma, gains joy connected with the Dhamma.
an11.11:3.15 | S: When they’re joyful, rapture springs up. When the mind is full of rapture, the body becomes tranquil. When the body is tranquil, they feel bliss. And when they’re blissful, the mind becomes immersed in samādhi. | T: In one who is joyful, rapture arises. In one whose mind is enraptured, the body grows calm. One whose body is calmed experiences ease. In one at ease, the mind becomes concentrated.
an11.11:3.16 | S: This is called a noble disciple who lives in balance among people who are unbalanced, and lives untroubled among people who are troubled. They’ve entered the stream of the teaching and developed the recollection of the Buddha. | T: “Of one who does this, Mahānāma, it is said: ‘Among those who are out of tune, the disciple of the noble ones dwells in tune; among those who are malicious, he dwells without malice; having attained the stream of Dhamma, he develops the recollection of the Buddha.’
an11.11:4.1 | S: Furthermore, you should recollect the teaching: | T: [2] “Then there is the case where you recollect the Dhamma:
an11.11:4.2 | S: ‘The teaching is well explained by the Buddha—apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves.’ | T: ‘The Dhamma is well taught by the Blessed One, to be seen here & now, timeless, inviting verification, pertinent, to be experienced by the observant for themselves.’
an11.11:4.3 | S: When a noble disciple recollects the teaching their mind is not full of greed, hate, and delusion. … | T: At any time when a disciple of the noble ones is recollecting the Dhamma, his mind is not overcome with passion, not overcome with aversion, not overcome with delusion.
an11.11:4.4 | P: ujugatamevassa tasmiṁ samaye cittaṁ hoti dhammaṁ ārabbha. | T: His mind heads straight, based on the Dhamma.
an11.11:4.5 | P: Ujugatacitto kho pana, mahānāma, ariyasāvako labhati atthavedaṁ, labhati dhammavedaṁ, labhati dhammūpasaṁhitaṁ pāmojjaṁ. | T: And when the mind is headed straight, the disciple of the noble ones gains a sense of the goal, gains a sense of the Dhamma, gains joy connected with the Dhamma.
an11.11:4.6 | P: Pamuditassa pīti jāyati, pītimanassa kāyo passambhati, passaddhakāyo sukhaṁ vediyati, sukhino cittaṁ samādhiyati. | T: In one who is joyful, rapture arises. In one whose mind is enraptured, the body grows calm. One whose body is calmed experiences ease. In one at ease, the mind becomes concentrated.
an11.11:4.7 | S: This is called a noble disciple who lives in balance among people who are unbalanced, and lives untroubled among people who are troubled. They’ve entered the stream of the teaching and developed the recollection of the teaching. | T: “Of one who does this, Mahānāma, it is said: ‘Among those who are out of tune, the disciple of the noble ones dwells in tune; among those who are malicious, he dwells without malice; having attained the stream of Dhamma, he develops the recollection of the Dhamma.’
an11.11:5.1 | S: Furthermore, you should recollect the Saṅgha: | T: [3] “Then there is the case where you recollect the Saṅgha:
an11.11:5.2 | S: ‘The Saṅgha of the Buddha’s disciples is practicing the way that’s good, direct, systematic, and proper. It consists of the four pairs, the eight individual persons. This is the Saṅgha of the Buddha’s disciples that is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of greeting with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world.’ | T: ‘The Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples who have practiced well… who have practiced straight-forwardly… who have practiced methodically… who have practiced masterfully—in other words, the four types (of noble disciples) when taken as pairs, the eight when taken as individual types—they are the Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples: deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, the incomparable field of merit for the world.’
an11.11:5.3 | S: When a noble disciple recollects the Saṅgha their mind is not full of greed, hate, and delusion. … | T: At any time when a disciple of the noble ones is recollecting the Saṅgha, his mind is not overcome with passion, not overcome with aversion, not overcome with delusion.
an11.11:5.4 | P: ujugatamevassa tasmiṁ samaye cittaṁ hoti saṅghaṁ ārabbha. | T: His mind heads straight, based on the Saṅgha.
an11.11:5.5 | P: Ujugatacitto kho pana, mahānāma, ariyasāvako labhati atthavedaṁ, labhati dhammavedaṁ, labhati dhammūpasaṁhitaṁ pāmojjaṁ. | T: And when the mind is headed straight, the disciple of the noble ones gains a sense of the goal, gains a sense of the Dhamma, gains joy connected with the Dhamma.
an11.11:5.6 | P: Pamuditassa pīti jāyati, pītimanassa kāyo passambhati, passaddhakāyo sukhaṁ vediyati, sukhino cittaṁ samādhiyati. | T: In one who is joyful, rapture arises. In one whose mind is enraptured, the body grows calm. One whose body is calmed experiences ease. In one at ease, the mind becomes concentrated.
an11.11:5.7 | S: This is called a noble disciple who lives in balance among people who are unbalanced, and lives untroubled among people who are troubled. They’ve entered the stream of the teaching and developed the recollection of the Saṅgha. | T: “Of one who does this, Mahānāma, it is said: ‘Among those who are out of tune, the disciple of the noble ones dwells in tune; among those who are malicious, he dwells without malice; having attained the stream of Dhamma, he develops the recollection of the Saṅgha.’
an11.11:6.1 | S: Furthermore, you should recollect your own ethical conduct, which is intact, impeccable, spotless, and unmarred, liberating, praised by sensible people, not mistaken, and leading to immersion. | T: [4] “Then there is the case where you recollect your own virtues: ‘(They are) untorn, unbroken, unspotted, unsplattered, liberating, praised by the observant, ungrasped at, conducive to concentration.’
an11.11:6.2 | S: When a noble disciple recollects their ethical precepts their mind is not full of greed, hate, and delusion. … | T: At any time when a disciple of the noble ones is recollecting virtue, his mind is not overcome with passion, not overcome with aversion, not overcome with delusion.
an11.11:6.3 | P: ujugatamevassa tasmiṁ samaye cittaṁ hoti sīlaṁ ārabbha. | T: His mind heads straight, based on virtue.
an11.11:6.4 | P: Ujugatacitto kho pana, mahānāma, ariyasāvako labhati atthavedaṁ, labhati dhammavedaṁ, labhati dhammūpasaṁhitaṁ pāmojjaṁ. | T: And when the mind is headed straight, the disciple of the noble ones gains a sense of the goal, gains a sense of the Dhamma, gains joy connected with the Dhamma.
an11.11:6.5 | P: Pamuditassa pīti jāyati, pītimanassa kāyo passambhati, passaddhakāyo sukhaṁ vediyati, sukhino cittaṁ samādhiyati. | T: In one who is joyful, rapture arises. In one whose mind is enraptured, the body grows calm. One whose body is calmed experiences ease. In one at ease, the mind becomes concentrated.
an11.11:6.6 | S: This is called a noble disciple who lives in balance among people who are unbalanced, and lives untroubled among people who are troubled. They’ve entered the stream of the teaching and developed the recollection of their ethical conduct. | T: “Of one who does this, Mahānāma, it is said: ‘Among those who are out of tune, the disciple of the noble ones dwells in tune; among those who are malicious, he dwells without malice; having attained the stream of Dhamma, he develops the recollection of virtue.’
an11.11:7.1 | S: Furthermore, you should recollect your own generosity: | T: [5] “Then there is the case where you recollect your own generosity:
an11.11:7.2 | S: ‘I’m so fortunate, so very fortunate. | T: ‘It is a gain, a great gain for me,
an11.11:7.3 | S: Among people full of the stain of stinginess I live at home with heart rid of stinginess, freely generous, open-handed, loving to let go, committed to charity, loving to give and to share.’ | T: that—among people overcome with the stain of possessiveness—I live at home, my awareness cleansed of the stain of possessiveness, freely generous, openhanded, delighting in being magnanimous, responsive to requests, delighting in the distribution of alms.’
an11.11:7.4 | S: When a noble disciple recollects their generosity their mind is not full of greed, hate, and delusion. … | T: At any time when a disciple of the noble ones is recollecting generosity, his mind is not overcome with passion, not overcome with aversion, not overcome with delusion.
an11.11:7.5 | P: ujugatamevassa tasmiṁ samaye cittaṁ hoti cāgaṁ ārabbha. | T: His mind heads straight, based on generosity.
an11.11:7.6 | P: Ujugatacitto kho pana, mahānāma, ariyasāvako labhati atthavedaṁ, labhati dhammavedaṁ, labhati dhammūpasaṁhitaṁ pāmojjaṁ. | T: And when the mind is headed straight, the disciple of the noble ones gains a sense of the goal, gains a sense of the Dhamma, gains joy connected with the Dhamma.
an11.11:7.7 | P: Pamuditassa pīti jāyati, pītimanassa kāyo passambhati, passaddhakāyo sukhaṁ vediyati, sukhino cittaṁ samādhiyati. | T: In one who is joyful, rapture arises. In one whose mind is enraptured, the body grows calm. One whose body is calmed experiences ease. In one at ease, the mind becomes concentrated.
an11.11:7.8 | S: This is called a noble disciple who lives in balance among people who are unbalanced, and lives untroubled among people who are troubled. They’ve entered the stream of the teaching and developed the recollection of generosity. | T: “Of one who does this, Mahānāma, it is said: ‘Among those who are out of tune, the disciple of the noble ones dwells in tune; among those who are malicious, he dwells without malice; having attained the stream of Dhamma, he develops the recollection of generosity.’
an11.11:8.1 | S: Furthermore, you should recollect the deities: | T: [6] “Then you should recollect the devas:
an11.11:8.2 | S: ‘There are the gods of the four great kings, the gods of the thirty-three, the gods of Yama, the joyful gods, the gods who love to create, the gods who control what is created by others, the gods of the Divinity’s host, and gods even higher than these. | T: “There are the Devas of the Four Great Kings, the Devas of the Thirty-three, the Devas of the Hours, the Contented Devas, the Devas who delight in creation, the Devas [Muses?] who wield power over the creations of others, the Devas of Brahmā’s Retinue, the devas beyond them.
an11.11:8.3 | S: When those deities passed away from here, they were reborn there because of their faith, ethics, learning, generosity, and wisdom. I, too, have the same kind of faith, ethics, learning, generosity, and wisdom.’ | T: Whatever conviction they were endowed with, so that—when falling away from this life—they re-arose there, the same sort of conviction is present in me as well.
an11.11:8.4 | P: Yathārūpena sīlena samannāgatā tā devatā ito cutā tatthūpapannā, mayhampi tathārūpaṁ sīlaṁ saṁvijjati. | T: Whatever virtue they were endowed with, so that—when falling away from this life—they re-arose there, the same sort of virtue is present in me as well.
an11.11:8.5 | P: Yathārūpena sutena samannāgatā tā devatā ito cutā tatthūpapannā, mayhampi tathārūpaṁ sutaṁ saṁvijjati. | T: Whatever learning they were endowed with, so that—when falling away from this life—they re-arose there, the same sort of learning is present in me as well.
an11.11:8.6 | P: Yathārūpena cāgena samannāgatā tā devatā ito cutā tatthūpapannā, mayhampi tathārūpo cāgo saṁvijjati. | T: Whatever generosity they were endowed with, so that—when falling away from this life—they re-arose there, the same sort of generosity is present in me as well.
an11.11:8.7 | P: Yathārūpāya paññāya samannāgatā tā devatā ito cutā tatthūpapannā, mayhampi tathārūpā paññā saṁvijjatī’ti. | T: Whatever discernment they were endowed with, so that—when falling away from this life—they re-arose there, the same sort of discernment is present in me as well.
an11.11:8.8 | S: When a noble disciple recollects the faith, ethics, learning, generosity, and wisdom of both themselves and the deities their mind is not full of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: At any time when a disciple of the noble ones is recollecting the conviction, virtue, learning, generosity, and discernment found both in himself and the devas, his mind is not overcome with passion, not overcome with aversion, not overcome with delusion.
an11.11:8.9 | S: At that time their mind is quite unswerving, based on the deities. | T: His mind heads straight, based on the (qualities of the) devas.

